  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nUpper high over the Four Corners will lead to dry weather locally\nover the next couple days. Surface high spanning across the\nSouthern Plains will maintain E/SE flow and a subtle cool air \nadvection pattern across Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas on\nMonday. This will keep temperatures slightly below normal for the\nstart of October, with lowland highs only reaching the mid-to- \nupper 70s again Monday afternoon. Surface pressure gradient \ntightens a bit Sunday afternoon, which will lead to low- end \nbreezy winds 15 to 25 mph.\n\nThe week ahead is a bit messy to forecast, given weak forcing \naloft and few day-to-day changes. Prevailing mid-latitude jet flow\nremains mostly zonal in nature across the northern U.S, not\naffecting our forecast area much at all. Weak upper high drifts \neast over the Southern Rockies on Tuesday, which will warm \ntemperatures a few degrees, putting us right near normal. This\nupper high will continue to sink into a developing low, developing\ndirectly over NM/TX midweek. Cooler temperatures aloft \nWednesday/Thursday will allow for some meager instability and \nslight chance for showers and thunderstorms. The problem is \nmoisture is forecast to remain quite low, with precipitable water \nonly 0.8-1.0\". There is plenty of moisture to our south, and we'll\nsee thunderstorm activity over Chihuahua through the week ahead. \nQuestion is whether or not that moisture can drift this far north.\nRight now, the answer is no and the forecast leans toward \ncontinued dry weather. NBM is starting to catch on to these low- \nend rain chances, so mentionable PoPs (10-30%) are now included \nfor Wednesday-Thursday. Stronger mid-latitude flow flushes all \nthis moisture out by next week.",
